      <geometry>
        <cylinder length="0.05" radius="0.101"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="mitt_tyre"/>
    </visual>
    <!-- Rim: visual only, no collision and no inertia - the tyre cylinder
           above owns both.

           It must stand PROUD of the tyre by a real margin. At 1.04x the tyre
           width the rim face was ~1 mm clear, which is sub-pixel at normal
           viewing distance and z-fought with the tyre. 1.20x gives 5 mm a
           side, which reads clearly and still looks like a hub rather than an
           axle stub.

           RENDERER DIFFERENCE, verified 2026-09-04. Gazebo paints this its own
           colour. RViz2's RobotModel display does NOT: it renders the geometry
           of every visual but applies ONE material per link, taken from the
           first visual - so in RViz the rim shows as a stepped hub in tyre
           black. Same applies to the LiDAR cap and camera lens in
           mitt_sensors.xacro.

           Left as is deliberately. The fix would be a child link per accent
           joined by a fixed joint, and that would add half a dozen decorative
           frames to a TF tree students are asked to read in the Week 5 lab.
           Cosmetics do not get to pollute the frame graph. -->
